/**
@page runtime_services Memory, Queue, and Threading

@section runtime_memory Memory Pool

sgl_memory_pool_initialize() registers one caller-owned region. The allocator
uses aligned first-fit blocks, coalesces adjacent free blocks, and serializes
allocation metadata. It never calls `malloc`, `calloc`, or `free` from the C
runtime.

The following operations allocate SGL memory:

- resize and rotate LUT creation;
- queue and thread-pool creation;
- parallel operation descriptors and selected resize scratch buffers;
- one-shot resize calls that receive a NULL LUT.

sgl_memory_pool_deinitialize() returns #SGL_FAILURE while any allocation is
live. Destroy thread pools, queues, and LUTs before deinitialization.

@section runtime_queue Queue Ownership

sgl_queue_t stores non-owning, non-NULL object pointers in FIFO order. Destroying
a queue releases only queue storage; it never releases enqueued objects.
sgl_queue_enqueue(), sgl_queue_dequeue(), and sgl_queue_peek() lock the queue.
sgl_queue_unsafe_enqueue() is intended for single-threaded queue preparation.

@code{.c}
sgl_queue_t *operations;
operation_t items[4];
sgl_size_t index;

operations = sgl_queue_create(4U);
if (operations != SGL_NULL) {
    for (index = 0U; index < 4U; ++index) {
        (void)sgl_queue_unsafe_enqueue(operations, &items[index]);
    }
}
@endcode

@section runtime_threadpool Thread-Pool Submission

sgl_threadpool_attach_routine() is synchronous: it returns after all callbacks
finish and restores operation pointers to the queue in completion order. The
submitting thread participates in processing. Use
sgl_threadpool_attach_routine_consuming() when the queue may be left empty; it
avoids the temporary completion queue allocation.

Callbacks can execute concurrently and must not assume a stable worker index.
Each operation object must remain alive until the attach call returns. The
cookie may be NULL, but any shared mutable state reachable through it requires
caller-provided synchronization.

@section runtime_image_pool Image Operation Pool Argument

Passing a valid pool allows an operation to divide sufficiently large images
into row ranges. Small workloads can still execute on the submitting thread to
avoid dispatch overhead. Passing #SGL_NULL always requests the single-thread
path and is the correct choice for callers that do not need a thread pool.
*/
